THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
Proof forests are at least as large as the skeptical fixed-point state they describe: a single accepted node may have exponentially many minimal proof trees (one distinct attacker-subset per combination of attackers-defeated-by-defenders), so the per-node label set can blow up even when the underlying grounded extension is polynomial-size. Because SRC_001 defines the extension itself as exactly those nodes carrying a non-empty proof-tree set and makes every accepted node carry its full minimal-proof set first-class, the construction does not just record a polynomial witness per node — it commits to materializing a potentially exponential object per node. This violates the stated requirement that the proposal preserve polynomial cost: the output representation alone can be exponential, independent of any later use.

TARGET:
Make warrant-chain provenance first-class in the label assignment: each accepted node is labelled with a set of minimal proof trees derived from committed roots, and the extension is the set of nodes having at least one such proof. This is a proof-theoretic reformulation of the grounded extension. Criticizable: the set of minimal proofs may be exponential; the definition of minimality may be ambiguous.